        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1073,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 1089,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151376,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1089,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable: </em></p>\r\n<p><em>LSIL : condyloma;VaIN I</em></p>\r\n<p><em>HSIL: VaIN II; VaIN III</em></p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1074,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1090,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151377,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1090,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LSIL (Vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia, grade I); HSIL (vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia, grade II; vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia, grade III)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1075,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151378,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal SILs arise in the squamous mucosa of the vagina and are most common in the upper&nbsp;third and fornices.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151379,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesions (LSILs) and high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesions (HSILs) are usually asymptomatic and often discovered during colposcopy for HPV-associated cervical lesions, as &gt;&nbsp;80% of cases co-occur or have a history of cervical SILs or squamous cell carcinoma {30161052; 33754436}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151380,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The incidence of vaginal SILs has been estimated at 0.6&ndash;1.5&nbsp;per 100&nbsp;000&nbsp;person-years, but the literature is sparse {32571683; 23580435}. The mean age of patients with vaginal HSIL is approximately 50&nbsp;years {33754436; 32571683; 34460120}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151381,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LSIL is associated with both low-risk and high-risk HPV types. It is the morphological manifestation of a productive, transient HPV infection and therefore tends to regress. HSIL is associated almost exclusively with high-risk HPV types and is the morphological manifestation of a transforming infection. HPV16 is the most prevalent type in both HSIL and LSIL, although its proportion increases with the grade of SIL {30561092; 34460120; 38373185}. Other relatively common types include HPV33, HPV52, and HPV18 {30561092}. Risk factors for vaginal SILs are similar to those for cervical SIL and include smoking, immunosuppression, and HIV; a history of cervical SIL is also a risk factor {36958755}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151382,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is similar to that of SILs of the cervix; see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/47\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Squamous intraepithelial lesions </em>&nbsp;<em>of the uterine cervix</em> for further details.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151383,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal SILs are difficult to recognize with the naked eye and can typically be appreciated on colposcopy after application of 3&ndash;5% acetic acid or Lugol&rsquo;s iodine solution. Vaginal SIL is often multifocal.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151384,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal SILs are divided into two-tiers using the grading system recommended by the Lower Anogenital Squamous Terminology (LAST) standardization project {22742517}. The histological features and the recommendations for p16 immunohistochemistry are similar to those described in the section on cervical SIL (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/47\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Squamous intraepithelial lesions of the uterine cervix</em>). For illustrations, see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/47\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Squamous intraepithelial lesions of the uterine cervix</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151385,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytological features are similar to those in the uterine cervix. See <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/47\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Squamous intraepithelial lesions of the uterine cervix</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1098,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151386,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1098,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1085,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1101,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151387,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1101,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The essential and desirable diagnostic criteria are as described in the section on cervical SILs (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/47\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Squamous intraepithelial lesions of the uterine cervix</em>).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1083,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1099,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151388,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1099,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1084,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1100,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151389,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 1100,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The risk of progression of HSIL (VaIN&nbsp;2 or&nbsp;3) to invasion is estimated to be about 2&ndash;6% (lower than in the cervix), with the highest risk in HSIL/VaIN&nbsp;3 {29185264; 32881786; 33754436; 36958755}; the risk of progression of LSIL is much lower. The risk of recurrence of SIL is significantly higher in immunocompromised patients {30161052}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:11:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]